From b31d27af69dbdcfd459859c1689c75f9fd054139 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: jjude80 <18168364+jjude80@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 21 Oct 2026 20:31:39 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 1/5] NEXA-424: give each issue its own execution workspace

An isolated execution workspace is one issue's worktree: one directory, one
branch, one index. A child of a decomposed parent inherited the parent's
workspace row, so six children of NEXA-415 were dispatched into the parent's
worktree and four runs were live in it at once. The cost was measured: a
whole-file write deleted another run's edits, git commit published every
staged path in the tree including another issue's, and the tree did not
build for reasons that belonged to neither run.

Two layers, because a creation-time rule alone does not cover an issue that
already points at somebody else's workspace.

1. Creation time (issues.ts). A child inherits the parent's mode and
   settings, but not the parent's workspace row when that row is an isolated
   workspace. A shared workspace is still inherited: sharing it is the point
   of it. A pinned existingBranch is stripped from inherited settings when
   the workspace is not bound, because a branch is the thing two issues
   must not share.

2. Dispatch time (heartbeat/workspaces.ts). The reuse request now carries
   the issue, the workspace mode and the workspace owner. Reusing an
   isolated workspace owned by a different issue is refused, and the refusal
   names the holder through conflictingWorkspaceOwnerIssueId. Recovery keeps
   its own workspace: a run that continues work already done in a tree is
   not a second issue joining it.

The refusal is loud. The queue preflight posts an issue comment naming the
holder issue, deduplicated by an HTML marker, so the run learns whose tree
it was about to write into instead of discovering it in a merge conflict.

Tests: execution-workspace-policy (50 cases), issues-service (32 workspace
cases), heartbeat-workspace-session (182 cases, including the new
conflict cases). Typecheck clean.
